      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate, in a prospective study, the potential for providing elective transurethral prostatectomy on a day-case basis, using new bipolar electrosurgical technology."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Forty men with confirmed bladder outlet obstruction caused by benign prostatic enlargement underwent plasma kinetic vaporization of the prostate (PKVP) in the Day Surgery Unit; they were scheduled to be discharged on the day of surgery with an indwelling catheter, returning for a trial removal of the catheter at 48 h, and followed up at 2 and 4 months after surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Thirty-four (85%) of the 40 patients were able to return home on the day of surgery. All patients, including the six who failed to return home as a day-case, successfully voided at 48 h; 78% felt the procedure was acceptable on a day-case basis. At 4 months the mean improvement in the International Prostate Symptom Score and Quality of Life score were 64% and 84%, respectively. The mean improvement in flow rate was 200-250%."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "PKVP effectively relieves prostatic obstruction, at least in the short-term (a longer term follow-up is required). The procedure can be delivered safely on a day-case basis and was very acceptable to the patients."
        }
      ]},
